#heading(numbering: none)[Brief Historical Notes] <back:notes>

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none, outlined: false)[Chapter I]

#source(263)
*@sec:martinelli-bochner.* Formula @eq:martinelli-bochner was obtained by
Martinelli @bib:Martinelli1938 and Bochner @bib:Bochner1943 independently and by
different methods. Theorem~@th:aronov-kytmanov was stated for the case of a ball
by Aronov @bib:Aronov1973a, and in the general case by Aronov and Kytmanov
@bib:Aronov1975. Theorem~@th:kytmanov-aizenberg was given by Kytmanov and
Aĭzenberg in @bib:Kytmanov1975b (see also @bib:Kytmanov1975a). The question of
functions representable by the integral @eq:martinelli-bochner was also examined
in the erroneous articles @bib:Serbin1971 and @bib:Serbin1975.

*@sec:rouche-principle.* The concept of the index of a surface with respect to a
point is due to Poincaré. The dynamic definition of the multiplicity of a zero
is encountered in papers by Segre @bib:Segre1938, Severi @bib:Severi1953, and
Martinelli @bib:Martinelli1955. Formula @eq:logarithmic-residue for $n = 2$ was
first obtained by Caccioppoli @bib:Caccioppoli1949. Here we mainly follow the
monographs @bib:Milnor1968 (see Appendix B) and @bib:Shabat1976b (§48). However,
we do not use the local theory of analytic functions in deriving the properties
of the multiplicity of a zero nor in proving the discreteness of a compact set
of zeros of a holomorphic mapping. Theorem~@th:rouche-principle (the Rouché
principle) is a particular case of the Poincaré-Bohl theory (see
@bib:Krasnoselskii1975, p.~14). See also @bib:Palamodov1967b about the
multiplicity of a zero of a holomorphic mapping. A generalization of the
logarithmic residue to the case of incomplete intersections is considered in
@bib:Solomin1977.

*@sec:leray-koppelman.* The Cauchy-Fantappiè formula was obtained by Leray in
@bib:Leray1959 (see also @bib:Aizenberg1964a, @bib:Aizenberg1970,
@bib:Khenkin1969, and @bib:Koppelman1967a). The Leray-Koppelman formula
@eq:generalized-cauchy-fantappie was mentioned by Roos @bib:Roos1974; however,
it was actually contained in Koppelman's earlier article @bib:Koppelman1967a.
Formula @eq:yuzhakov-roos was derived by Yuzhakov @bib:Yuzhakov1975c,
@bib:Yuzhakov1973 and Roos @bib:Roos1974, and the formula
@eq:cauchy-fantappie-mapping by Roos @bib:Roos1974. Formula
@eq:fundamental-integral-formula, which generalizes the Leray-Koppelman formula
@eq:generalized-cauchy-fantappie, is contained in @bib:Aizenberg1977a. For the
Cauchy-Fantappiè formula @eq:cauchy-fantappie we can ask a question analogous to
the one answered in @sec:martinelli-bochner for formula @eq:martinelli-bochner:
is a function holomorphic if it is representable by formula @eq:cauchy-fantappie
with respect to its values on $partial D$? The answer to this question is no
(Kytmanov).

#source(264)
*@sec:cauchy-formula.* Integrals of the form @eq:skeleton-residue were first
studied by Didon in @bib:Didon1873 for $n = 2$ and $phi equiv 1$, but he did not
discover their connection with the multiplicity of a zero.
Theorem~@th:caccioppoli-martinelli-sorani and
Proposition~@prop:martinelli-sorani for $n = 2$ were proved by Caccioppoli
@bib:Caccioppoli1949 ($phi equiv 1$) and Martinelli @bib:Martinelli1955, while
for $n > 2$ they were proved by Sorani @bib:Sorani1960. The multiplicity of an
intersection of analytic surfaces and generalizations of the logarithmic residue
for $n = 2$ were considered also by Wirtinger @bib:Wirtinger1937 and Bergman
@bib:Bergman1959. Theorem~@th:yuzhakov-kuprikov, which includes
Theorem~@th:skeleton-boundary and formula @eq:yuzhakov-roos as special cases,
was proved in @bib:Yuzhakov1973. This theorem is a generalization of the
integral representation of Sorani @bib:Sorani1966. Theorem~@th:yuzhakov is
contained in @bib:Yuzhakov1975a and @bib:Yuzhakov1973, and Theorem~@th:tsikh was
proved in @bib:Tsikh1975. The formulas @eq:yuzhakov-roos and
@eq:skeleton-residue were duplicated in @bib:Bescheron1975.

*@sec:andreotti-norguet.* The Andreotti-Norguet formula @eq:andreotti-norguet
was published in @bib:Andreotti1964 and @bib:Norguet1974 (see also
@bib:Martinelli1975); it has important applications in the theory of
$q$-pseudoconvex domains. The proof given is due to Aĭzenberg
@bib:Aizenberg1978; formulas @eq:andreotti-norguet-fantappie,
@eq:andreotti-norguet-koppelman, the problem in @pt:andreotti-norguet-residue,
and cases 1)–4) were taken from @bib:Aizenberg1977a and @bib:Aizenberg1978.
Proposition~@prop:bolotov is due to Bolotov.

*@sec:kernels.* The concepts of the Bergman boundary, the Bergman kernel
function (for $h equiv 1$), the Bergman metric are encountered in a number of
papers by Bergman (see, for example, @bib:Bergman1931 about the Bergman
boundary). The concept of a ring boundary was introduced by Shilov (see, for
example, @bib:Gelfand1960), and he proved the existence and uniqueness of this
boundary for the space of maximal ideals of a normed ring.
Theorem~@th:shilov-boundary-existence and Corollary~@cor:shilov-boundary-complex
are taken from @bib:Fuks1963 (§15) and @bib:Aizenberg1966a. The example of a
domain of holomorphy for which the Shilov boundary and the Bergman boundary have
different dimensions is due to Aĭzenberg (see @bib:Fuks1963, §16).
Theorem~@th:gleason was stated by Gleason @bib:Gleason1962, Theorems~@th:bungart
and~@th:bungart-szego by Bungart @bib:Bungart1964a, @bib:Bungart1964b, and
Theorem~@th:shilov-boundary-converse by Aĭzenberg.

*@sec:martinelli-bochner-koppelman.* Theorem~@th:koppelman for forms of type
$(0, q)$ was obtained by Koppelman @bib:Koppelman1967b, while in the case of
forms of type $(p, q)$ it was obtained by Aronov and Dautov @bib:Aronov1972b and
by Roos @bib:Roos1972; however, there is an error in the latter.
Theorems~@th:koppelman-generalized-derivatives and~@th:koppelman-weighted are
due to Dautov. The idea of introducing a function $phi(zeta, z)$ with the
property that $phi(z, z) equiv 1$ into the integral formulas was used by
Aĭzenberg @bib:Aizenberg1964a, @bib:Aizenberg1970. Formula
@eq:martinelli-bochner-koppelman was extended to forms in the real space $RR^n$
by Norguet in the lecture notes @bib:Norguet1974. If we represent a
$dbar$-closed form by formula @eq:martinelli-bochner-koppelman, then the second
term in @eq:martinelli-bochner-koppelman disappears. Conversely, suppose that a
form $alpha$ can be represented by the Martinelli-Bochner-Koppelman formula
@eq:martinelli-bochner-koppelman without the second term. Is $alpha$
$dbar$-closed in this case? In other words, is the representability of a form
$alpha$ by formula @eq:martinelli-bochner-koppelman without the second term a
characteristic property of $dbar$-closed forms? The following is a particular
case of this problem: is a function holomorphic if it is representable by a
#source(265) Martinelli-Bochner integral (see @sec:martinelli-bochner)? Kytmanov
gives a positive answer to this question in @bib:Kytmanov1976b. Tarkhanov
obtained an analogous result for closed forms in $RR^n$ @bib:Tarhanov1976.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none, outlined: false)[Chapter II]

*@sec:leray-modifications.*
Propositions~@prop:modified-cauchy-fantappie–@prop:aizenberg are taken from
@bib:Aizenberg1964a and @bib:Aizenberg1970. Formula @eq:linearly-convex-formula
was obtained for convex domains by Leray @bib:Leray1959. It has been extended to
regular linearly convex domains @bib:Aizenberg1967a, @bib:Aizenberg1967b. The
Bochner formula @eq:ellipsoid-formula was mentioned in @bib:Bochner1943, while
the Bochner-Ono formula @eq:bochner-ono was given in @bib:Bochner1943 (for a
neighborhood of zero) and in @bib:Ono1956. The contents of
@pt:hermitian-defining-function are due to Aĭzenberg.

*@sec:bergman-weil.* A formula for functions holomorphic in an analytic
polyhedron of a very general form was published in a number of papers by Bergman
(see, for example, @bib:Bergman1936). The result in this section is due to Weil
@bib:Weil1935, but the idea for the proof presented is due to Norguet
@bib:Norguet1960, @bib:Norguet1961.

*@sec:strictly-pseudoconvex.* Theorems~@th:khenkin-ramirez
and~@th:khenkin-estimate were stated by Khenkin @bib:Khenkin1968,
@bib:Khenkin1969. A result close to Theorem~@th:khenkin-ramirez was obtained
independently, but somewhat later, by Ramírez de Arellano @bib:Ramirez1969 (see
also @bib:Koranyi1971 and @bib:Ovrelid1971); see @bib:Hormander1967 in
connection with Lemma~@lem:local-convexification.

*@sec:n-circular.* The Temlyakov integrals of the first and second kinds were
obtained by Temlyakov in 1954 and definitely formulated in @bib:Temlyakov1957
and @bib:Temlyakov1958. Formula @eq:temlyakov-representation is due to Opial and
Siciak @bib:Opial1963; see @bib:Aizenberg1959b, @bib:Bavrin1966a,
@bib:Bavrin1966b, and @bib:Li1959 for other extensions of the Temlyakov integral
representation to the case of $n > 2$ complex variables. See @bib:Sheinov1967
concerning extension to circular domains. A number of sources deal with
applications of the Temlyakov integrals to diverse problems in function theory
(see @bib:Aizenberg1958–@bib:Aizenberg1959b, @bib:Aizenberg1962 and
@bib:Sheinov1962). Temlyakov integrals are the subject of @bib:Braun1974, but
there is an error there, and in @bib:Aizenberg1976d it is explained that
Temlyakov integral representations cannot be extended to $n$-circular domains
that are not convex. Theorem~@th:aizenberg-szego and Examples 1) and 2) are
taken from @bib:Aizenberg1964b; see also @bib:Aizenberg1961b and
@bib:Aizenberg1973a. Example 3) is due to Zinov'ev @bib:Zinovev1974; see also
@bib:Borodin1969. The results in @pt:extended-szego are due to Aĭzenberg
@bib:Aizenberg1964b, @bib:Aizenberg1973a. A construction can be carried out
similarly for the Bergman, Poisson, and Schwarz kernels @bib:Zinovev1971b. Other
Szegö and Bergman kernels are computed in @bib:Egorychev1973,
@bib:Egorychev1975, @bib:Egorychev1977, @bib:Zinovev1974, and @bib:Zinovev1973.
See the article @bib:Aizenberg1976c of Aĭzenberg and Egorychev about extension
to unbounded domains. Formula @eq:de-leeuw-aizenberg-gindikin is implicitly
contained in de Leeuw's article @bib:DeLeeuw1957. More precisely, he mentioned
the existence of such a formula, but did not write out its kernel. This was done
by Aĭzenberg in @bib:Aizenberg1964b for $n = 2$ under certain conditions
(ensuring a unique choice of $Delta(k)$) on the boundary $partial D$, and then
by Gindikin in @bib:Gindikin1966 for any $n$ and without any conditions on
$partial D$. He found (see @bib:Gindikin1966) that there is a problem in
integral geometry which leads to formula #source(266)
@eq:de-leeuw-aizenberg-gindikin. It is required to recover a function on an
$n$-dimensional torus if its integrals over the closed totally geodesic
hypersurfaces on this torus are known. This problem is connected with Fourier
series in the same way the familiar Radon problem is connected with Fourier
integrals.

*@sec:schwarz-kernel.* See @bib:Vladimirov1976a, Chapter~III, on the role of
holomorphic functions with nonnegative real part in mathematical physics.
Example~@exm:polydisk is due to Korányi and Pukánszky @bib:Koranyi1963; a
related result can be found in the article @bib:Vladimirov1974b of Vladimirov
and Drozhzhinov, where holomorphic functions in a polydisk with nonnegative
imaginary parts are studied. Example~@exm:generalized-unit-disk is taken from
@bib:Vladimirov1974a and @bib:Vladimirov1977. The remaining results in this
section were obtained by Aĭzenberg and Dautov @bib:Aizenberg1976b. In
@bib:Vladimirov1969 and @bib:Vladimirov1977 Vladimirov gave characteristic
integral formulas for functions with nonnegative imaginary part that are
holomorphic in certain tubular (unbounded) domains. We do not know whether these
results fit in the scheme of @sec:schwarz-kernel when it is generalized to
unbounded domains.

Much work relating to integral representations in the most divergent settings
could not be included in this book. We mention the results of Bitsadze on
analogues of the Cauchy integral for three-dimensional space @bib:Bitsadze1969.
A lot of work has been done on recovering a holomorphic function from its values
on sufficiently small (in various senses) sets (results in the spirit of the
Goluzin-Krylov formula in @bib:Privalov1950 (Chapter~II, formula (5.9:3)), see
@bib:Dautov1976, @bib:Znamenskii1975–@bib:Znamenskii1978, @bib:Mesis1967,
@bib:Mkrtchyan1976a, @bib:Mkrtchyan1976b and @bib:Patil1974). Znamenskiĭ
@bib:Znamenskii1978 has considered integral representations in the spirit of the
Vostretsov formula (see @bib:Privalov1950) for a family of holomorphic functions
that is uniformly bounded on compact subsets of a domain $D$.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none, outlined: false)[Chapter III]

*@sec:statement.* As far back as 1830 Jacobi @bib:Jacobi1830 actually used the
concept of a multiple residue as the coefficient of the negative first powers in
a multiple Laurent series. Attempts at generalizing the concept of a residue to
functions of two complex variables are encountered in work of Cauchy, Didon
@bib:Didon1873, Picard @bib:Picard1886a, @bib:Picard1886b, and Poincaré
@bib:Poincare1886. However, the memoir @bib:Poincare1887 of Poincaré should be
regarded as the beginning of the theory of multidimensional residues; there he
generalized the Cauchy integral theorem to functions of two complex variables,
considered the periods of rational functions of two variables (after proving
that they can be expressed in terms of the periods of abelian integrals), and
used double residues to substantiate a generalization found by Stieltjes
@bib:Stieltjes1885 of the Lagrange expansion for two variables. The theory of
multiple residues was subsequently extended in work of Lefschetz
@bib:Lefschetz1916, de Rham @bib:DeRham1936, Segre @bib:Segre1938, Severi
@bib:Severi1953, Leray @bib:Leray1959, Dolbeault @bib:Dolbeault1956,
@bib:Dolbeault1957, and others (see the remarks on
@sec:alexander-pontryagin[§§14]–@sec:local-residues[19]).

#source(267)
*@sec:alexander-pontryagin.* The idea of applying the Alexander-Pontryagin
duality is due to Martinelli, who used it in extending the Cauchy integral
formula to holomorphic functions of $n$ complex variables and to $(n + l)$-fold
integrals ($0 <= l <= n - 1$) @bib:Martinelli1953, as well as in generalizing
the logarithmic residue @bib:Martinelli1955. In
@bib:Yuzhakov1960–@bib:Yuzhakov1966 Yuzhakov developed this idea as proposed by
Ivanov in connection with the general theory of residues and with residues of
rational functions.

*@sec:de-rham.* A proof of the de Rham theorem can be found in @bib:DeRham1955
and @bib:Hormander1966. The formulation presented here is taken from
@bib:Leray1959. Theorem~@th:serre of Serre is contained in @bib:Serre1953.
Theorem~@th:de-rham-residues on residues was stated by Yuzhakov in
@bib:Yuzhakov1975c and @bib:Yuzhakov1976.

*@sec:leray-residues.* The exposition in @sec:leray-residues mainly follows
Leray's book @bib:Leray1959. The operator $delta^(-1)$ inverse to the Leray
coboundary operator was actually used by Poincaré @bib:Poincare1887. The theory
of residues on complex analytic manifolds was largely created by Leray (see
@bib:Leray1959). Its subsequent development and generalization was fostered by
Norguet @bib:Norguet1959a, @bib:Norguet1959b, @bib:Norguet1971, Sorani
@bib:Sorani1963, Dolbeault @bib:Dolbeault1970–@bib:Dolbeault1972, Griffiths
@bib:Griffiths1966–@bib:Griffiths1970, Herrera @bib:Herrera1970a,
@bib:Herrera1970b, @bib:Herrera1970c, King @bib:King1974, @bib:King1975, and
others @bib:Gutenmakher1972, @bib:Krasnov1972, @bib:Kantor1971,
@bib:VanDerCorput1965, @bib:Gordon1974, @bib:Sebastiani1970, @bib:Segre1938,
@bib:Shih1970, @bib:Solomin1977. Theorem~@th:froissart on the Froissart
decomposition is contained in @bib:Fotiadi1965, Part~II, §3.
Proposition~@prop:griffiths was proved in @bib:Griffiths1969,
Proposition~@prop:yuzhakov-coboundary in @bib:Yuzhakov1970, and
Theorem~@th:pedan in @bib:Pedan1973 and @bib:Pedan1974.

*@sec:cohomological-reduction.* A method for finding the multiple residue class
of semimeromorphic forms (Theorem~@th:gelfand-shilov-leray) is described in
@bib:Gelfand1958 and @bib:Leray1959, and the case of a simple residue class is
analyzed in @bib:Pham1967 (Chapter~III, §4.3). Theorem~@th:griffiths-reduction
is contained in @bib:Griffiths1969, and Theorem~@th:leinartas in
@bib:Leinartas1978.

*@sec:two-variables.* The main results in this section were published by
Yuzhakov in @bib:Yuzhakov1962a, @bib:Yuzhakov1962b, and @bib:Yuzhakov1972b.
Theorem~@th:poincare-abelian-integrals was first mentioned by Poincaré in
@bib:Poincare1887. Theorem~@th:projective-curve-betti was given in
@bib:Yuzhakov1972a, and Theorems~@th:tsikh-projective and~@th:tsikh-n-variables
in @bib:Tsikh1973 and @bib:Tsikh1977a.

*@sec:local-residues.* This section was written on the basis of
@bib:Yuzhakov1964, @bib:Yuzhakov1966, and @bib:Yuzhakov1972a. Multidimensional
residues also form the subject of @bib:ElZein1974, @bib:Herrera1971,
@bib:Aronszajn1977, @bib:Carrell1978, @bib:Coleff1977, @bib:Dolbeault1977,
@bib:ElZein1971, @bib:Griffiths1978, and @bib:Mebkhout1975.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none, outlined: false)[Chapter IV]

*@sec:implicit-functions.* Hermite gave a simple derivation of the
Bürmann-Lagrange series @eq:burmann-lagrange with the help of residues in 1865.
The first generalization of the Lagrange expansion to functions of two variables
(Theorem~@th:stieltjes-poincare-good) was indicated by Stieltjes
@bib:Stieltjes1885 and proved with the help of the two-dimensional residues of
Poincaré @bib:Poincare1887. Good @bib:Good1960, Sack @bib:Sack1965a, and others
(see @bib:Goursat1929 and @bib:Sturrock1960) have considered
Theorem~@th:stieltjes-poincare-good for $n >= 2$, as well as certain other
modifications of it.

#source(268)
The formula for inverting a vector-valued function, generalizing
@eq:burmann-lagrange, was obtained by Percus @bib:Percus1964 by operator
methods. Formula @eq:inversion-series was obtained in @bib:Yuzhakov1975a by
using logarithmic residues. Results close to
Proposition~@prop:branched-inversion were obtained in @bib:Kuprikov1975.
Generalizations of the Lagrange expansion to arbitrary implicit functions
(Theorems~@th:yuzhakov-implicit-series and~@th:yuzhakov-degenerate,
Propositions~@prop:implicit-taylor and~@prop:implicit-one-variable) can be found
in @bib:Yuzhakov1974–@bib:Yuzhakov1975b.

A formula for inverting a power series (the formula for the $n$th derivative of
the inverse function) was found by Sylvester @bib:Sylvester1854 in 1854, and
rediscovered in @bib:Ostrowski1957. Its generalization for systems of power
series (Theorem~@th:cayley-sylvester-sack) was obtained for $n = 2$ and 3 by
Cayley @bib:Cayley1856 and Sylvester @bib:Sylvester1857. For $n >= 2$
Theorem~@th:cayley-sylvester-sack was proved by Sack @bib:Sack1965b by operator
methods. Theorem~@th:yuzhakov-bolotov was proved by Bolotov and Yuzhakov in
@bib:Bolotov1978. Yuzhakov obtained Theorem~@th:generalized-schwarz-lemma and
Theorems~@th:yuzhakov-convergence–@th:inversion-remainder on estimates of the
domain of convergence and the remainder for a multiple Lagrange series.

Other estimates of the domain of convergence for inversion of a holomorphic
mapping were determined by Shoĭkhet @bib:Shoikhet1976.

*@sec:nonlinear-systems.* Some results on systems of nonlinear algebraic
equations were found in the last century by Jacobi @bib:Jacobi1835, Hermite
@bib:Hermite1852, and Kronecker. Theorems~@th:aizenberg-power-sums
and~@th:aizenberg-simple-system were obtained by Aĭzenberg @bib:Aizenberg1976a,
@bib:Aizenberg1977a, @bib:Aizenberg1978, Theorems~@th:aizenberg-tsikh
and~@th:aizenberg-tsikh-weighted by Aĭzenberg and Tsikh @bib:Aizenberg1977b,
Theorem~@th:bolotov-waring by Bolotov, and Propositions~@prop:real-roots
and~@prop:roots-in-ellipsoid by Aĭzenberg. Example~@exm:roots-in-ellipse was
computed at the request of the authors by M.~Mkrtchyan. We did not discuss the
various iteration methods for the approximate solution of systems of nonlinear
equations; a detailed survey of these methods and a bibliography can be found in
@bib:Ortega1970.

*@sec:multiplicity.* Theorem~@th:kushnirenko was published in
@bib:Kushnirenko1975a, and a modification of it for the Milnor numbers in
@bib:Kushnirenko1975b. Theorem~@th:nondegenerate-multiplicity was stated and
proved on the basis of a completely analogous theorem of Bernstein
@bib:Bernstein1975 on the number of roots of a system of Laurent polynomials.
Theorem~@th:multiplicity-resultant was proved for $n = 2$ in @bib:Tsikh1976, and
for $n > 2$ in @bib:Yuzhakov1978. Theorem~@th:tsikh-yuzhakov is also contained
in @bib:Yuzhakov1978. Theorem~@th:weighted-principal-part was obtained by Tsikh
@bib:Tsikh1977b.

*@sec:generating-functions.* Theorem~@th:egorychev-yuzhakov was stated in
@bib:Egorychev1972 and @bib:Egorychev1974b. The method of computing
combinatorial sums with the help of residues is thoroughly worked out in the
books @bib:Egorychev1974a and @bib:Egorychev1977 of Egorychev. A great variety
of examples are analyzed in these references, and a number of problems of
combinatorial nature arising in group theory, graph theory, and function theory
are solved.

#heading(level: 2, numbering: none, outlined: false)[Chapter V]

*@sec:hartogs-bochner.* Much work has dealt with generalization of the classical
theorems of Hartogs, Osgood and Braun (Theorem~@th:hartogs-osgood-braun) and
Bochner and Severi (Theorem~@th:bochner): see @bib:Baikov1969,
@bib:Vinogradov1968, @bib:Andreotti1972, @bib:Bochner1943, @bib:Bremermann1959,
@bib:Fichera1957, @bib:Kohn1965b, @bib:Martinelli1961, @bib:Nirenberg1972,
@bib:Schaeffer1970, @bib:Severi1953, #source(269) @bib:Weinstock1969, and
@bib:Weinstock1970. Theorem~@th:aronov-kytmanov-extension is due to Aronov and
Kytmanov @bib:Aronov1975, @bib:Aronov1976b, and
Theorem~@th:kytmanov-aizenberg-extension and
Corollary~@cor:spherical-harmonics-criterion to Kytmanov and Aĭzenberg
@bib:Kytmanov1975b. Theorems 7 and 10, which concern the holomorphic extension
of functions in the class $L^p (partial D)$ into $D$, are taken from Khenkin and
Chirka @bib:Khenkin1975a (Chapter~I). Khenkin showed that the assertion of
Theorem~@th:kytmanov-aizenberg-extension is valid also for functions in
$L^1 (partial D)$ (here it is necessary to speak of $H^1 (D)$ in place of
$A_C (D)$.) Theorem~@th:agranovskii-valskii-stout for the case of a ball is
contained in Agranovskiĭ and Val'skiĭ @bib:Agranovskii1971 (see also
@bib:Nagel1976), and for the general case in Stout @bib:Stout1977. In
@bib:Agranovskii1971 it is actually proved that $f in C(partial D)$ extends
holomorphically into the ball $D$ if the integrals of $f$ over the sections of
$partial D$ by complex lines are equal to zero.
Lemma~@lem:one-dimensional-extension was proved by Kytmanov, following a
suggestion of Khenkin. Theorem~@th:aizenberg-dautov is due to Aĭzenberg and
Dautov @bib:Aizenberg1976b.

Theorem~@th:weil-expansion and Corollary~@cor:polynomial-weil-runge were
formulated by Weil. Linear convexity for $n = 2$ was introduced in an equivalent
form by Behnke and Peschl in @bib:Behnke1935; it was considered and investigated
in detail in the general case by Aĭzenberg @bib:Aizenberg1967a,
@bib:Aizenberg1967b. A closely related but not equivalent concept was
investigated by Martineau in @bib:Martineau1966a.
Theorem~@th:martineau-aizenberg was erroneously announced by Martineau in
@bib:Martineau1962 for compact sets and domains that are linearly convex in the
Martineau sense. Valid results for compact convex sets and convex domains were
obtained by Martineau @bib:Martineau1966a and Aĭzenberg @bib:Aizenberg1966b and,
in the form presented in @sec:hartogs-bochner, by Aĭzenberg @bib:Aizenberg1967a.
Theorem~@th:aizenberg-partial-fractions and
Corollary~@cor:aizenberg-simple-fractions are taken from @bib:Aizenberg1967b.
Theorem~@th:trutnev is due to Trutnev @bib:Trutnev1973, and
Theorem~@th:znamenskii to Znamenskiĭ. See @bib:Aizenberg1972,
@bib:Aizenberg1971b, @bib:Aizenberg1968, @bib:Zinovev1971a, @bib:Makarova1970,
@bib:Makarova1973, @bib:Stepanenko1973, @bib:Stepanenko1976,
@bib:Trutnev1972a–@bib:Trutnev1973, @bib:Yuzhakov1971b and
@bib:Martineau1962–@bib:Martineau1968 on other applications of the concept of
linear convexity and on its properties. Much work has been done on diverse
applications of integral representations to the study of spaces of holomorphic
functions: see @bib:Aizenberg1967a, @bib:Gindikin1962, @bib:Zakharyuta1967,
@bib:Zakharyuta1971, @bib:Mityagin1961, @bib:Mityagin1971, @bib:Trutnev1972a,
@bib:Trutnev1973, @bib:Khavin1966–@bib:Khenkin1969, @bib:Khenkin1975a,
@bib:Kerzman1972, @bib:Kohn1963, @bib:Martineau1966a–@bib:Martineau1968,
@bib:Stein1973 and @bib:Venugopalkrishna1972.

*@sec:dbar-problem.* The Levi problem, whose solution is given by Oka's theorem,
was posed in 1910 in @bib:Levi1910. Oka proved his theorem for domains in $CC^2$
in 1942. Later, proofs appeared for $CC^n$ by Oka @bib:Oka1953, Bremermann
@bib:Bremermann1954, and Norguet @bib:Norguet1954. The best-known proofs are due
to Grauert @bib:Grauert1958 and Hörmander @bib:Hormander1966. @sec:dbar-problem
was written by Dautov on the basis of a manuscript of Khenkin (1973), where
Oka's main theorems are derived in an elementary fashion from integral formulas
for convex domains. Theorem~@th:finite-codimension, which is the basis of
@sec:dbar-problem, was proved by Romanov and Khenkin @bib:Romanov1971. It
refines previous results of Khenkin @bib:Khenkin1970, Grauert and Lieb
@bib:Grauert1970, Øvrelid @bib:Ovrelid1971, Kerzman @bib:Kerzman1971, and Lieb
@bib:Lieb1972. Theorems~@th:khenkin-skoda and~@th:dautov-khenkin, which take
into account the different behavior of the right-hand side of the
$dbar$-equation along the complex tangent and the complex normal, were proved by
Khenkin @bib:Khenkin1977b, Skoda @bib:Skoda1976, and Dautov and #source(270)
Khenkin @bib:Dautov1977. The formula presented in this section (formula
@eq:dbar-solution-operator) for the solution of the $dbar$-problem was obtained
in @bib:Dautov1977 for the purpose of proving Theorem~@th:dautov-khenkin.
Theorems~@th:khenkin-skoda and~@th:dautov-khenkin form the basis for
Theorem~@th:khenkin-skoda-zeros (a description of the sets of zeros of functions
of Nevanlinna class; Khenkin @bib:Khenkin1977b and Skoda @bib:Skoda1976) and
Theorem~@th:dautov-khenkin-zeros (a description of the sets of zeros of
functions of Nevanlinna-Dzhrbashyan class; Dautov and Khenkin @bib:Dautov1977).
Other formulas for the solution of the $dbar$-equation, along with estimates of
them and applications, can be found in @bib:Polyakov1972, @bib:Khenkin1969,
@bib:Khenkin1970, @bib:Khenkin1971b, @bib:Khenkin1972, @bib:Ovrelid1971,
@bib:Severi1953 and @bib:Stout1976. A more detailed survey concerning the
$dbar$-problem is given in @bib:Khenkin1977a and @bib:Khenkin1975a.

*@sec:orthogonal-forms.* The first description of smooth forms orthogonal to
holomorphic functions in strictly pseudoconvex domains was obtained by Dautov
@bib:Dautov1972a (see also @bib:Aizenberg1975a). The corresponding extension in
the generalized sense is a consequence of the uniform estimates of Khenkin
@bib:Khenkin1970 for the $dbar$-operator (see @bib:Khenkin1977a).
Theorem~@th:dautov-extension was proved by Dautov according to a suggestion of
Khenkin, and Theorem~@th:khenkin-extension was proved by Khenkin
@bib:Khenkin1977b. Theorem~@th:dbar-exact-extension is actually contained in
@bib:Khenkin1977a. Theorems~@th:dautov-multiply-connected, @th:dautov-c2,
and~@th:aizenberg-envelope are Theorems 6.2, 8.1, and 7.1, respectively, in
@bib:Aizenberg1975a with refinement of certain formulations (see also
@bib:Aizenberg1973b, @bib:Dautov1972b and @bib:Dautov1972d).
Theorem~@th:khenkin-multiply-connected was communicated by Khenkin.
Theorem~@th:dautov-logarithmic-residue and
Corollary~@cor:cauchy-fantappie-kernels were obtained by Dautov. For $n = 2$
Corollary~@cor:cauchy-fantappie-kernels coincides with Theorem 13.2 in
@bib:Aizenberg1975a; for $n > 2$ a general form of integral representation
different from that in Corollary~@cor:cauchy-fantappie-kernels is given in
Theorem 13.4 of @bib:Aizenberg1975a.

The "tangential" Cauchy-Riemann operator $dbar_b$ was introduced by Kohn
@bib:Kohn1965a and Kohn and Rossi @bib:Kohn1965b. It is also defined for forms
in $C_(p, q)^((1)) (partial D)$ for any $p$, $0 <= p <= n$, and not just for
$p = n$, as in @sec:orthogonal-forms.@pt:lewy-equation. The Lewy equation
@eq:lewy-equation was considered by him in @bib:Lewy1956 and @bib:Lewy1957.
Theorems~@th:khenkin-lewy and~@th:khenkin-nonsolvable are due to Khenkin. In the
case when $n = 2$ and $D$ is a ball, Theorem~@th:khenkin-lewy is just a
reformulation of results of Sato and Greiner, Kohn, and Stein @bib:Greiner1975.
Theorem~@th:khenkin-nonsolvable is a refinement of the classical assertions of
Lewy @bib:Lewy1957 and Hörmander @bib:Hormander1963 on the existence of
unsolvable Lewy equations.

Proposition~@prop:jump-decomposition is a theorem on jumps for forms. The first
such assertions can be found in Aĭzenberg @bib:Aizenberg1973b and Andreotti and
Hill @bib:Andreotti1972 (see also @bib:Aizenberg1975a).

*@sec:algebraic-singularities.* The expansion @eq:rational-weil-series was noted
for polynomials and rational functions by Aĭzenberg.
Theorem~@th:yuzhakov-linear-factors was proved by Yuzhakov @bib:Yuzhakov1972a.
Proposition~@prop:mikhailova, along with certain other conditions for uniqueness
of the expansion @eq:rational-weil-series, were obtained by Mikhaĭlova
@bib:Mikhailova1972. Yuzhakov suggested the idea for constructing a basis for
the space $A(CC^n without T)$, where $T = {z: P(z) = 0}$, $P$ a #source(271)
polynomial, by approximating the domain $CC^n without T$ by Weil polyhedra and
applying Proposition~@prop:mikhailova to them. This idea was implemented
(Theorem~@th:algebraic-basis) in Mikhaĭlova's article @bib:Mikhailova1972. The
simplified proof presented here for Theorem~@th:algebraic-basis was suggested by
Zakharyuta. There is a generalization of Theorem~@th:algebraic-basis to the case
of pseudopolynomials and of certain classes of entire functions in
@bib:Mikhailova1973.
